  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Dividing by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The roots are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Exactly one is positive, and hence admissible.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math> is strictly decreasing and takes every positive value once. The admissible t therefore corresponds to exactly one real x. Answer B.</p>"
